    "result_text": "Both tests pass and the build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented a structured address form for the owner-create endpoint while keeping the flat `address` input fully backward-compatible.\n\n**What changed (6 files):**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added `addressLine1` (optional first line, preferred when present) and `addressLine2` (optional) to the `OwnerFields` schema, and removed `address` from the required list so structured-only owners bind and validate.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` columns with plain getters/setters so the structured values persist and round-trip through `GET`. MapStruct auto-maps them (no mapper edit needed).\n\n3. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 Added `address_line1`/`address_line2` columns (the test DB uses `ddl-auto=none`).\n\n4. **`db/hsqldb/data.sql`** \u2014 Appended the two new positional column values (`NULL, NULL`) to the seed owner rows.\n\n5. **`NormalizeOwnerAddress.java`** \u2014 Extracted the existing normalization (trim, collapse whitespace, upper-case, expand `ST/RD/AVE`) into a small reusable helper. When `addressLine1` is present, both lines are normalized and `address` is composed as line 1 (plus a single space and line 2 when line 2 is present); otherwise the flat `address` is normalized in place.\n\n6. **`RequireOwnerFields.java`** \u2014 Replaced the flat-only `address` check with a small `requireAddress` helper that accepts either a non-blank `addressLine1` or a non-blank `address`.\n\n**Structural-impact notes:** The address-reading rules (household hash, postcode validation, locality) read `postcode`/`city`, which are top-level fields supplied identically in both forms, so they needed no changes. New behaviour was added as small cohesive units \u2014 a private `normalize` helper and a private `requireAddress` helper \u2014 rather than growing existing methods, and I avoided editing the large MapStruct mapper by letting field-name auto-mapping carry the new fields.",
